course: dictionary
lesson: 10066
title: Words for cloth and making
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: A textile is any cloth or fabric that is woven, knitted or made from threads, such as cotton, wool or silk. Look at the words around it to check. In «In 1833 a British law banned children under nine from working in most textile mills», the word mills tells you that textile things are made in a factory.
explain: Textiles can also mean a school subject or craft where you design and make things from fabric. Try the swap check: put your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Textile can also come before another noun to describe it, as in textile industry, textile mill and textile design. In «textile mill», textile is like a describing word, telling you which kind of mill it is.
fact: A textile is any cloth or fabric that is woven, knitted or made from threads.
fact: Cotton, wool and silk are all textiles.
fact: «Textiles» can also mean a school subject or craft where you design and make things from fabric.
fact: «Textile mill» means a factory where cloth is made.
fact: In 1833 Britain's Factory Act banned children under nine from working in most textile mills.
exercise: «In 1833 a British law banned children under nine from working in most textile mills.» What is a textile mill? | A shop that sells tins | A school where children learn to read | A farm for growing wheat | *A factory where cloth is made
exercise: Which of these is a textile? | A tin of soup | A bag of coal | A wooden spoon | *A scarf woven from wool
exercise: Mia says, «In textiles lessons, I sewed a cushion cover from cotton.» What is textiles here? | A sport played in a gym | A type of weather | *A craft where you design and make things from fabric | A cooking class with ovens
test: Natural textiles are made from materials that come from plants or animals. What does natural mean here? | Made in a laboratory | *Coming from plants or animals | Very cheap | Shiny and smooth
test: «Children as young as six worked long hours in mills and mines.» What did the 1833 law stop? | Children working on farms only | Children working in all shops | Children going to school | *Children under nine working in most textile mills
exam: Which sentence uses textile as a describing word before a noun? | The cloth was made of cotton and silk, so it was a textile | You must check every word you read | The factory closed in 1833 and never reopened | *She works in the textile industry
